ID: B2-Ch05b-Similar
TEXT:

 "I remember you saying something similar before our battle with the troll..." Leila writes. The blue glyphs hang in the air between us, sharp and steady.

"That was different," I tell her. "The troll is dead. The beasts are dead. I don't see what cards they have left to play this late in the tournament."

"Really?" Leila's fingers trace fresh light. "You see no way the organizers might guarantee your execution in the next round?"

"Not a single one."

"Alright, then," she writes, letting the word fade into nothing.

A runner hurries across the sand to hand the announcer a fresh slip of parchment. The man scans the ink for a second, clears his throat, and raises his voice over the rumble of the stands.

"I shall now announce the contenders for the opening duel," the announcer calls out. "This contest is to the death. Combat continues until only one gladiator remains breathing. Surrender is strictly forbidden."

"Get ready," Leila writes.

"Ready for what?"

"The fighters are—" the announcer bellows, lifting the sheet high.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Disarmed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Disarmed)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Disarmed
TEXT:

 "—Barry and Daren!" the announcer finishes.

"Oh," I say.

The iron portcullis in front of my gate groans and rattles upward into the stone. Across the arena floor, the gate to Daren's division lifts at the exact same pace.

"Will the two contenders step forward into the ring?" the announcer's voice carries over the crowd, flat and bored. "Thank you."

"Oh," I say again. My mind goes completely blank.

"Good luck," Leila writes, her expression dead serious.

I step out onto the packed sand.

Calm down. No reason to panic. I have scraped through every impossible corner on this island so far, and this is just another corner. Deep breaths. Do not panic. Just think. Think...

Daren walks toward the center of the pit with long, easy strides. He looks infuriatingly relaxed. Why? Because he knows he can flatten me inside ten seconds? No, that is not Daren. He has an angle. He has already found the loophole, and I am out here hyperventilating instead of solving it.

We meet in the middle of the arena. Daren offers me a wide, blinding grin.

Then he lets his broadsword drop into the dirt, tosses his shield down with a heavy clang, and throws both hands straight into the air.

"I surrender!" Daren roars at the top of his lungs. "I cannot fight this man! How could I possibly hope to prevail?"

"This match is to the death, healer!" the announcer shouts from his podium. "Even if the man is your comrade—"

"Look at those muscles!" Daren bellows over him, gesturing wildly at my chest. "What sane man would dare cross blades with such a monster?"

A ripple travels through the stands, followed by an explosion of laughter.

"Those arms could crush my skull like a ripe melon between bare palms!" Daren continues, his grin widening into pure theatrical mockery. "Behold the sheer breadth of his back! Gods have mercy on my wretched soul!"

Very funny, Daren.

Still, credit where it is due. Surrender. Simple, brilliant, and right in plain sight.

The crowd is still roaring with laughter when the iron gate below the royal box grinds open. The ambassador of Ollendor strides out onto the sand, his fine silk robes fluttering, his face twisted in fury.

"What is the meaning of this insolence?" the ambassador shrieks. The laughter in the stands sputters out. "Why are you standing there? This is a duel to the death! Surrender is not a path!"

"Well," Daren says, folding his arms with an easy smirk. "What are you going to do about it?"

The ambassador turns purple. His glare snaps to me.

"You there!" he points a trembling finger. "Peasant! Your adversary has cast aside his steel. Strike him down this instant, or face the full penalty of the crown!"

choice(""If I'm the peasant, explain why the crown's man just walked onto someone else's execution to give orders."", B2-Ch05b-Peasant, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Peasant)
choice(""Sit down before you rupture something the crown still needs. The stands can wait."", B2-Ch05b-Doctor, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Doctor)
choice("Finish off Daren", B2-Ch05b-Finish, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Finish)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Peasant
TEXT:

 "If I'm the peasant, explain why the crown's man just walked onto someone else's execution to give orders."

I throw my voice straight toward the upper tiers so every spectator can catch the words.

"I speak for Ollendor, you lowborn mongrel!" the ambassador shrieks. "You are the peasant!"

"No, you are the peasant!" I shoot back.

"You are the peasant!" he screams, the veins in his neck bulging against his collar.

The stands lose all restraint. The entire amphitheater erupts into howls of laughter.

"Hey, Daren," I say casually. "I think I finally found someone easier to bait than Flower."

"Are you mocking me?" the ambassador spits.

"Of course he is mocking you," Daren says. "What did you honestly expect? That you could storm onto the sand, yell at the top of your lungs, and make us slaughter each other on command?"

"Disobey me and I will have you both executed where you stand!"

"No, you will not," Daren replies, his deep voice carrying clearly across the arena. "Standard arena regulations contain no mandate forcing fighters to butcher one another. We have violated a single event rule. Under Varathian law, the only penalty you can levy is to brand us undesirables and cast us into the penalty round. Those are the rules."

"I care nothing for your rules!" the ambassador roars. "I make the rules here! Obey, or you die before sunset!"

A low, uneasy rumble rolls through the tiers. The ambassador ignores the shift in the crowd and keeps shouting.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Shunned, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Shunned)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Doctor
TEXT:

 "Sit down before you rupture something the crown still needs. The stands can wait."

I project my voice directly up toward the crowded stone rows.

"I have no need of a physician!" the ambassador screams. "I command you to slay this man now! This very instant!"

"Which instant?" I ask.

"This instant!"

"Do you mean this exact second, or the one when you started shouting?" I scratch my chin. "Because if you need him dead two seconds ago, I will need to pick up time magic first."

Laughter rolls through the amphitheater, loud and unrestrained.

"Are you mocking me?" the ambassador spits.

"Of course he is mocking you," Daren says. "What did you honestly expect? That you could storm onto the sand, yell at the top of your lungs, and make us slaughter each other on command?"

"Disobey me and I will have you both executed where you stand!"

"No, you will not," Daren replies, his deep voice carrying clearly across the arena. "Standard arena regulations contain no mandate forcing fighters to butcher one another. We have violated a single event rule. Under Varathian law, the only penalty you can levy is to brand us undesirables and cast us into the penalty round. Those are the rules."

"I care nothing for your rules!" the ambassador roars. "I make the rules here! Obey, or you die before sunset!"

A low, uneasy rumble rolls through the tiers. The ambassador ignores the shift in the crowd and keeps shouting.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Shunned, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Shunned)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Finish
TEXT:

 "Fine, fine, I'll finish him off," I say.

I step forward, keep my dagger firmly sheathed in its scabbard, and halt right in front of Daren. With a straight face, I deliver a gentle tap to the center of his breastplate.

Daren clutches his chest, lets out an agonizing groan, and hurls himself backward into the dirt with the most melodramatic death thrash imaginable.

"It is done!" I shout toward the royal box, pitching my voice to carry to the highest seats. "The mighty healer is slain!"

The stands collapse into hysterical laughter. The ambassador's face darkens to a deep, dangerous crimson.

"Are you mocking me?" he roars.

"Of course we are mocking you," Daren grunts from the sand, casually rolling back onto his feet. "What did you honestly expect? That you could wave your hands, shout some threats, and make us turn on each other?"

"Disobey me and I will have you both executed where you stand!" the ambassador shrieks.

"No, you will not," Daren replies, his deep voice cutting through the noise. "Standard arena regulations contain no mandate forcing fighters to butcher one another. We have violated a single event rule. Under Varathian law, the only penalty you can levy is to brand us undesirables and cast us into the penalty round. Those are the rules."

"I care nothing for your rules!" the ambassador roars. "I make the rules here! Obey, or you die before sunset!"

A low, uneasy rumble rolls through the tiers. The ambassador ignores the shift in the crowd and keeps shouting.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Shunned, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Shunned)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Shunned
TEXT:

 "Did you truly believe those parchment laws carried any weight?" the ambassador bellows, his voice cracking with rage. "This entire arena was constructed for one purpose: to ensure your deaths! You were never meant to walk out of this pit alive! The whole spectacle is a farce! Do not convince yourselves you have a prayer of survival merely because you survived two pathetic waves. I am the ambassador of Ollendor! If I give the word, my guard will string you up before this entire coliseum, and not a single soul will raise a finger to stop it! That is my authority! Now slaughter each other like the dogs you are, or I will mount your severed heads on pikes alongside every friend you hold dear!"

The quiet muttering in the stands turns to a sharp hiss of boos. The jeering swells with every sentence he shrieks, spreading from section to section until thousands of voices drown out his threats.

"Off with the ambassador's head!" a spectator roars from the middle tier.

The shout catches like dry timber. A dozen voices take it up, then a hundred, then the whole amphitheater, hammering the words into a rhythmic, deafening chant.

"Off with his head! Off with his head! Off with his head!"

The arrogance drains from the ambassador's face, replaced by a pale, twitching dread as sweat beads across his brow.

"How does that sit with you, ambassador?"

Eiden's voice floats through the air, crisp and clear, drifting from the empty sand right behind the nobleman.

"Damn it, not him again," Daren mutters under his breath.

"Is that... Eiden?" The ambassador whips around, eyes darting across bare dirt.

"How does it feel to hear thousands of voices demanding your throat at once?" Eiden asks mildly. "Look upon these people, ambassador. Do you imagine your noble pedigree would shield you if you crossed their path in an alley? Do you think they would hesitate for a heartbeat to tear you apart if the gates opened?"

A heartbeat passes in silence.

"These are the very souls you have spent a lifetime despising," Eiden continues. "You brushed them aside as vermin, creatures beneath your royal notice. Do your convictions hold when the consequences stare you down? What will you choose, ambassador? The sands are running out."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Departure, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Departure)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Departure
TEXT:

 I can picture Eiden's serene, closed-eyed smirk as he floats somewhere in the empty air, watching the nobleman gnaw at his fingernails, desperate for a way to quell the mob without losing face. No clever exit presents itself.

"I have reconsidered!" the ambassador announces at last, shouting toward the stands with all the bravado he can muster. "We shall abide by the ancient laws! These two contenders are hereby stripped of standing and branded undesirables! If they outlast the penalty round, their freedom is granted as promised! Let the remaining duels proceed!"

He turns on his heel and marches toward the exit tunnel, putting distance between himself and the jeering crowd as fast as his legs can manage.

Daren stoops to retrieve his sword and shield from the dirt, then turns his gaze toward the empty patch of sand where the stillwater spoke.

"So, how is the view, Eiden?" Daren asks the open air. "Enjoying the performance?"

Only the wind answers.

"You know, I was reflecting on something," Daren goes on, unfazed by the silence. "During my travels across the eastern continent, I visited traveling troupes that brought spectators directly into the act. Seeing as you are such a devoted patron, I thought we might invite some audience participation. What do you say?"

Daren waits a second, listening.

"Wonderful," Daren says. "Heads up!"

In a single fluid motion, he whips his broadsword across the air with terrifying speed. The steel flashes straight through the empty space where Eiden had stood, whistling through thin air before sinking into the hard-packed sand twenty yards behind it.

Daren lets out a small, disappointed breath and jogs over to yank his blade from the ground.

With the ambassador gone, the official brings our match to a close, declaring me the victor by forfeit. I turn back toward the division holding cells, but the announcer calls out from his rail. He informs me that the tournament card listed an immediate death duel against a fighter from the sixth division, but adds that as a recognized undesirable, I hold the legal right to decline all duels until the final penalty melee.

choice("Refuse the duel", B2-Ch05b-Refuse, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Refuse, v_b2_ch5_spare = 0)
choice("Accept the duel", B2-Ch05b-Accept, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Accept)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Accept
TEXT:

 I accept the duel. Taking this gladiator on alone is far safer than facing him in the penalty melee when thirty fighters will be swinging at once.

I signal the announcer. Across the sand, a heavy portcullis grinds open, and my opponent strides out into the sunlight. I recognize him immediately—one of the sharpest killers in the sixth division, easily on par with the veterans from the first. He is tall as Daren and broad as Bruce, clad in hardened leather harness. Instead of a sword, he grips a massive double-edged battleaxe in both hands.

The horn will blow the second he reaches striking distance. The decree demands a death match, but as an undesirable, the kingdom's execution laws no longer bind me. Killing him ends the threat permanently, but shattering a knee or crippling a tendon will take him off the board just as cleanly.

choice("Aim to kill him", B2-Ch05b-Kill, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Kill)
choice("Aim to incapacitate him", B2-Ch05b-Incapacitate, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Incapacitate)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Kill
TEXT:

 I have no reason to offer this man mercy. Look at the thick scars on his forearms—this is not his first blood sport. If our positions were reversed, he would take my head without blinking.

The question is execution. Do I open with a disarm, or strike straight for an unarmored gap? Rush him with speed before he sets his stance, or wait out his heavy swings and counter when he overextends? I need a clean, calculated sequence before the steel crosses.

choice("Take him by surprise and disarm him", B2-Ch05b-Tactic1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic1)
choice("Take him by surprise and attack him directly", B2-Ch05b-Tactic2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic2)
choice("Wait for him to make mistakes, then disarm him", B2-Ch05b-Tactic3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic3)
choice("Wait for him to make mistakes, then attack him directly", B2-Ch05b-Tactic4, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic4)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Incapacitate
TEXT:

 There is no reason to take his life. I have no quarrel with him; I only need him off the field so I do not have to fight him in the final melee. Break a leg, sever a tendon, take the weapon—incapacitation is enough.

The question is execution. Do I open with a disarm, or strike straight for an unarmored gap? Rush him with speed before he sets his stance, or wait out his heavy swings and counter when he overextends? I need a clean, calculated sequence before the steel crosses.

choice("Take him by surprise and disarm him", B2-Ch05b-Tactic1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic1)
choice("Take him by surprise and attack him directly", B2-Ch05b-Tactic2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic2)
choice("Wait for him to make mistakes, then disarm him", B2-Ch05b-Tactic3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic3)
choice("Wait for him to make mistakes, then attack him directly", B2-Ch05b-Tactic4, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Tactic4)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Tactic1
TEXT:

