I hold my ground, tracking the arc of his swings, waiting for the right opening to strike.

Dodging a two-handed battleaxe wielded by a seasoned pit fighter demands total focus. The wide swings whistle past my face, churning the sand, each blow carrying enough weight to shear through a wooden post. The long curve of the scimitar lets me deflect the closest strikes while slipping past his reach.

The moment he overextends on a sweeping chop, his right flank opens wide. I step inside the recovery arc and thrust the scimitar into the side of his chest. The shallow bite is enough to break his nerve; he leaps backward in panic, trips on his own boots, and crashes into the dirt, losing his grip on the axe. I kick the weapon away and hold the steel to his throat.

}
