ID: B2-Ch05b-Mercy
TEXT:

 "Hold!" the gladiator shouts, his voice cracking with terror. "I surrender!"

"This match is to the death, warrior," the announcer's voice comes down cold and flat from the rail. "Yielding is forbidden."

"Please..." The man's chest heaves as he stares up at me from the dirt. "Have mercy... I have family... I don't want to die in the sand! Please!"

"Kill him! Kill him! Kill him!"

The chant erupts from the lower tiers and sweeps through the entire coliseum, thousands of voices screaming for blood.

The roar shakes the stone benches. I stand over the fallen fighter with my blade pressed against his windpipe, weighing the seconds.

choice("Kill him", B2-Ch05b-Spare, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Spare, v_b2_ch5_spare = 0)
choice("Stab him in the right leg, but spare his life", B2-Ch05b-Spare, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Spare, v_b2_ch5_spare = 1)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Spare
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch5_speaker,1) if (v_b2_ch5_spare == 1)
achievement("Public speaker",v_ac_b2_ch5_speaker)
#if(v_b2_ch5_spare == 0) {
 I draw the steel back a few inches, line up the angle, and drive the point clean through the center of his chest.

The gladiator's body stiffens once, then goes completely slack in the sand.

A savage roar of approval rises from the stands as the announcer officially records the kill and names me the victor. I sheath my weapon, walk back through the open portcullis, and let the heavy iron gate rattle shut behind me. I lean against the stone wall in silence, watching the remaining matches unfold.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_spare == 1) {
 I tune out the chanting crowd, shift my grip, and drive the blade deep into the thick meat of the gladiator's right thigh.

The man lets out a ragged scream, clutching his bleeding leg. The wound is clean, but the severed muscle will keep him from putting weight on that foot for the rest of the day. He will not be swinging an axe in the penalty round.

I wipe the steel on my pant leg, turn my back, and walk toward the division gate.

"What do you think you are doing?" the announcer yells from the podium.

"Returning to my cell," I call back without slowing down. "The match is over. I won."

"The match is over when I declare it over!" the announcer's voice trembles with anger. "Get back out there and finish the execution!"

"No."

"Have you lost your senses?" the announcer shouts. "That man is no comrade of yours! You never saw his face before this morning. What reason do you have to spare him?"

"I am not butchering a stranger for your circus," I say. "My objective was putting him out of the final melee, and he is out. If you want his throat cut, come down here and do it yourself."

"I will withhold your victory until you deliver the finishing blow!"

"Award him the victory, then," I say, waving a dismissive hand. "I don't care. I am done."

A chorus of boos rains down from the stone benches. Curses and jeers pelt me with every step toward the portcullis.

"Shut your mouths!" I roar, whirling on the stands.

The sheer venom in my voice catches the front rows off guard. The noise dies down into a startled murmur as thousands of spectators stare down at the lone gladiator in the sand.

"What is broken inside you people?" I shout, pitching my voice to echo through every archway. "Is this your idea of an afternoon out? Watching men get disemboweled and mauled by wild beasts for sport? How sick do you have to be to find joy in slaughter? You disgust me. Does watching people bleed in the dirt make your own miserable lives feel easier to bear? Because the second you walk out through those gates, the exact same pathetic existence is waiting for you out on the street! And you have earned every scrap of it. The cruelty you cheer for in this pit is the exact same cruelty your nobles feed you every single day. You have forfeited the right to complain about a single hardship in your lives. Go home, look in a mirror, and find some shame before you bray like mindless sheep again. I am going back to my cell. I am done with you fools."

A stunned silence hangs in the air for two heartbeats—and then the amphitheater erupts into a deafening wall of fury. Shouts, obscenities, and hurled fruit bounce off the stone walls.

I turn my back on the mob and stride into the division pen without a second glance. The iron gate drops behind me with a solid thud.

"Wow," Leila writes in glowing blue letters as I catch my breath. "That was... quite the speech."

"Yeah," I grunt.

We stand side by side and watch the rest of the matches in silence.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Undesirables, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Undesirables)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Refuse
TEXT:

 I take full advantage of my undesirable brand and refuse the duel. There is no sense in burning stamina before the final melee. Every ounce of energy I save right now goes toward surviving whatever meat grinder they have planned for the end.

I inform the announcer of my refusal and turn back to my cell. The iron portcullis grinds shut behind me, and I settle against the cold masonry to watch the remaining duels.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Undesirables, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Undesirables)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Undesirables
TEXT:

 The remaining duels pass in a blur of quick clashes and cautious maneuvering. A clear pattern takes shape in the matchmaking. Neither Daren, Hadrik, nor I receive another summons to the sand. Kate gets called twice, but she exercises her undesirable privilege and refuses each time. More noticeably, the death matches cease entirely. Fighters trade superficial cuts, clash steel for a few passes, or yield before striking a single blow whenever the gap in skill proves obvious.

By the time the third round draws to a close, the ambassador's scheme is transparent. He is hoarding living bodies, preserving every gladiator he can find so they can overwhelm us in the penalty melee.

The victor of the third round is a swordsman from the seventh division who accomplished nothing remarkable beyond facing three consecutive cowards who threw down their weapons on entry. Once the man claims his pouch of coin, the horn blows for the assembly.

"Will all undesirables step forward and form a line facing the arena ranks?" the announcer calls out.

#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 0 && v_b2_ch5_wilhelm == 0) {
 Daren, Hadrik, Kate, and I walk out to the center of the sand, standing shoulder to shoulder with weapons drawn.

"Now, who among our brave champions steps forward to contest the—" The announcer cuts short as Leila and Bruce march out from the fourth division pen, striding across the dirt to join our line.

"Halt!" the announcer shouts. "You stand on the wrong side of the ring! Neither of you carry the undesirable brand!"

"Not yet!" Bruce roars back, thumping his breastplate.

"Are you telling me," the announcer's voice cracks in disbelief, "that you voluntarily take the undesirable brand after surviving the tournament, solely to stand beside these outcasts?"

"The true fighters of the fourth division stand united until the final horn sounds!" Bruce bellows.

Bruce glares straight across at Wilhelm as the words leave his mouth. The assassin scowls, averts his eyes, and stays rooted in the shadows of the pen.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 0 && v_b2_ch5_wilhelm == 1) {
 Daren, Hadrik, Kate, and I walk out to the center of the sand, standing shoulder to shoulder with weapons drawn.

"Now, who among our brave champions steps forward to contest the—" The announcer cuts short as Leila and Bruce march out from the fourth division pen, striding across the dirt to join our line.

"Halt!" the announcer shouts. "You stand on the wrong side of the ring! Neither of you carry the undesirable brand!"

"Not yet!" Bruce roars back, thumping his breastplate.

"Are you telling me," the announcer's voice cracks in disbelief, "that you voluntarily take the undesirable brand after surviving the tournament, solely to stand beside these outcasts?"

"The true fighters of the fourth division stand united until the final horn sounds!" Bruce bellows.

Bruce glares straight across at Wilhelm. For a couple of tense seconds, the assassin stares at the sand. Then he exhales, steps out of the pen, and walks over to take his place in our line.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 1 && v_b2_ch5_wilhelm == 0) {
 Daren, Kate, and I walk out to the center of the sand, standing shoulder to shoulder with weapons drawn.

"Now, who among our brave champions steps forward to contest the—" The announcer cuts short as Hadrik, Leila, and Bruce march out together, crossing the open dirt to form up on our flank.

"Halt!" the announcer shouts. "You stand on the wrong side of the ring! None of you carry the undesirable brand!"

"Not yet!" Bruce roars back, thumping his breastplate.

"Are you telling me," the announcer's voice cracks in disbelief, "that you voluntarily take the undesirable brand after surviving the tournament, solely to stand beside these outcasts?"

"The true fighters of the fourth division stand united until the final horn sounds!" Bruce bellows.

Wilhelm glares across at Bruce. The assassin scowls, tightens his jaw, and remains where he is.

"And what of you, dwarf?" the announcer demands, pointing at Hadrik.

"You know me," Hadrik grins, loosening his shoulders. "I never turn down a proper brawl!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_creatures == 1 && v_b2_ch5_wilhelm == 1) {
 Daren, Kate, and I walk out to the center of the sand, standing shoulder to shoulder with weapons drawn.

"Now, who among our brave champions steps forward to contest the—" The announcer cuts short as Hadrik, Leila, and Bruce march out together, crossing the open dirt to form up on our flank.

"Halt!" the announcer shouts. "You stand on the wrong side of the ring! None of you carry the undesirable brand!"

"Not yet!" Bruce roars back, thumping his breastplate.

"Are you telling me," the announcer's voice cracks in disbelief, "that you voluntarily take the undesirable brand after surviving the tournament, solely to stand beside these outcasts?"

"The true fighters of the fourth division stand united until the final horn sounds!" Bruce bellows.

Wilhelm glares across at Bruce. After a moment of hesitation, the assassin breaks his stance and walks across the sand to join our line.

"And what of you, dwarf?" the announcer demands, pointing at Hadrik.

"You know me," Hadrik grins, loosening his shoulders. "I never turn down a proper brawl!"

}
