choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Seal, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Seal)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Seal
TEXT:

 "Hey, Tyrath!" Arraka's voice shrills from the lower seats to our left. "Long time, no see! Remember me? It's your dearest friend, Arraka!"

Flower leaps from the royal tier, twin jets of bright fire erupting from the soles of her boots to cushion her landing. She hits the sand lightly and jogs over to join our line.

"Arraka..." Tyrath growls, his slit eyes locking onto the copper amulet resting against Flower's collarbone.

"Did you get a makeover or something?" Arraka cackles from the pendant. "What happened to those dazzling molten lava scales? Where is that fearsome fiery aura you used to show off? Did old age catch up with you at last? AHAHAHAHA! So much for supreme power! You must loathe looking in the mirror!"

"How did you shatter the seal?" Tyrath demands.

"The cavern seal?" Arraka mocks. "Seriously? You never realized I broke loose? Did you sleep straight through the still winter? Were you hibernating in a mud hole? Boy, do I have stories for you!"

"The seal," Tyrath repeats, his voice dropping to a dangerous rumble. "How did you break it?"

"That shoddy ward started crumbling after the first thousand years," Arraka replies. "Once the cracks widened, all I had to do was wait for a vessel to wander in, and BAM! Host acquired. Well, technically we share the body now, but still. What did you honestly expect? You needed a whole coalition of lessathi and human mages just to trap me in the first place, and you thought your pathetic little seal would hold forever? Don't make me laugh! You only caught me back then because I had just been exiled from the magical plane and barely understood this world's rules. In a proper rematch today, I would turn you into charcoal."

"You threaten me from inside a trinket of glass and metal?" Tyrath sneers.

"Shatter the amulet, dragon, and we can settle the score right here."

"Rot in your cage," Tyrath spits. "I came for vengeance, and to teach an insignificant insect the cost of defying me."

choice(""Wait, are you talking about me?"", B2-Ch05b-Talking, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Talking)
choice(""Didn't you lose against me the last time, though?"", B2-Ch05b-Lose, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Lose)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Talking
TEXT:

 "Wait, are you talking about me?"

"Yes, you, miserable wretch!" Tyrath roars. "Silence your tongue and activate those magical enhancements so I may prove how fragile you truly are!"

"Well, about that..." I say, holding up a palm. "I can't actually activate those stats anymore."

"You refuse to face me at full strength?"

"No, you don't understand," I tell him. "I literally cannot use those stats right now. Could you perhaps come back in a few months after I study the theory?"

"If you refuse to draw your power," Tyrath snarls, "I will slaughter your companions one by one before your eyes. Once they are ash, I will level this entire city, then reduce every human settlement on this island to cinders. I will finish today what should have ended ten thousand years ago. Your vermin kind should never have crawled from the caves."

A massive fireball detonates directly against the dragon's chest, followed by a rapid volley of secondary concussions that shroud him in smoke.

Flower hovers two hundred feet in the air, riding roaring columns of fire from her boots, and pumps a victorious fist.

"Nice work stalling him, Barry!" Flower calls down, giving me a bright thumbs-up.

"Watch out!" I yell up. "The dragon is immune to fire!"

"Fire immunity does not negate kinetic force," Illuna's voice cuts in from the same throat. "The shockwave cracks his hide regardless."

"You heard her!" Flower grins. "One more blast coming up!"

She gathers a spinning orb of white-hot flame between her hands and hurls it straight into Tyrath's shoulder, detonating with a thunderous crack.

"So that is the overgrown lizard you mentioned," Hadrik says with a broad grin, his eyes bright with battle hunger. "Let's see what he takes to crack!"

Hadrik transforms into a colossal eagle, beating massive feathered wings as he climbs into the sky. Flower pauses her barrage. The eagle maneuvers above Tyrath's blind spot, shifts back to dwarf form in mid-air, and drives a hammer-fist straight down into the dragon's spine.

The bone-shattering thud carries all the way to the sand, dropping Tyrath twenty feet through the air. Hadrik shifts back to eagle form and banks hard, circling for another pass.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Cone, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Cone)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Lose
TEXT:

 "Didn't you lose against me the last time, though?"

"You survived only because an underground trap interrupted our duel!" Tyrath roars. "It matters not. I am here to correct the record. Silence your tongue and activate those magical enhancements so I may prove how fragile you truly are!"

"Well, about that..." I say, holding up a palm. "I can't actually activate those stats anymore."

"You refuse to face me at full strength?"

"No, you don't understand," I tell him. "I literally cannot use those stats right now. Could you perhaps come back in a few months after I study the theory?"

"If you refuse to draw your power," Tyrath snarls, "I will slaughter your companions one by one before your eyes. Once they are ash, I will level this entire city, then reduce every human settlement on this island to cinders. I will finish today what should have ended ten thousand years ago. Your vermin kind should never have crawled from the caves."

A massive fireball detonates directly against the dragon's chest, followed by a rapid volley of secondary concussions that shroud him in smoke.

Flower hovers two hundred feet in the air, riding roaring columns of fire from her boots, and pumps a victorious fist.

"Nice work stalling him, Barry!" Flower calls down, giving me a bright thumbs-up.

"Watch out!" I yell up. "The dragon is immune to fire!"

"Fire immunity does not negate kinetic force," Illuna's voice cuts in from the same throat. "The shockwave cracks his hide regardless."

"You heard her!" Flower grins. "One more blast coming up!"

She gathers a spinning orb of white-hot flame between her hands and hurls it straight into Tyrath's shoulder, detonating with a thunderous crack.

"So that is the overgrown lizard you mentioned," Hadrik says with a broad grin, his eyes bright with battle hunger. "Let's see what he takes to crack!"

Hadrik transforms into a colossal eagle, beating massive feathered wings as he climbs into the sky. Flower pauses her barrage. The eagle maneuvers above Tyrath's blind spot, shifts back to dwarf form in mid-air, and drives a hammer-fist straight down into the dragon's spine.

The bone-shattering thud carries all the way to the sand, dropping Tyrath twenty feet through the air. Hadrik shifts back to eagle form and banks hard, circling for another pass.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Cone, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Cone)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Cone
TEXT:

 Tyrath recovers with a snarl of pure fury. Before Flower can weave another fireball, the dragon vanishes from the sky and snaps into existence directly in front of her, raking razor talons across the air.

Flower reacts instantly, discharging a blast of flame from her left palm that hurls her body sideways, clearing the talons by inches. Tyrath blinks again, reappearing behind her back. This time Illuna seizes control; twin high-pressure water jets erupt from her hands, smashing into the dragon's snout while thrusting her clear of his jaws.

Down on the sand, Kate conjures a massive, dense cone of ice above her head, pouring concentrated magical energy into its structure.

"Kate, that will not work," Daren warns, stepping beside her with sword drawn. "Remember the woods? Your ice cones barely scratched his scales. Raise an ice platform and loft me into the sky—my enchanted blade can pierce him!"

"This is not the woods," Kate replies, her jaw set hard. "I know the full scale of what we face. I have no luxury to ration power. I hit him with everything I have, even if it leaves me unconscious in the sand."

"Damn it, listen to reason!" Daren shouts, but Kate surrounds herself in a protective cage of ice and lifts off from the arena floor, ascending into the open air to secure a clean firing line.

The ice cone she shapes is unlike anything she has cast before. Dense, translucent blue-white, and honed to a razor tip, it carries the sheen and hardness of tempered elven steel. The air around the projectile hums and shivers with raw frost energy. The cone pivots on its own axis, tracking the dragon's teleportation signatures through the sky.

Tyrath blinks twice more in pursuit of Flower, but Hadrik intercepts him in eagle form, shifts to a dwarf in mid-plummet, and smashes a heavy fist straight into the dragon's jaw.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Stunned, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Stunned)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Stunned
TEXT:

 The punch leaves the dragon dazed for a crucial heartbeat.

Kate releases the spell. A concussive blast of compressed air roars behind the ice cone as it rockets across the sky at terrifying speed. The steel-hard tip strikes Tyrath dead center in the breast, punching clean through thick scales.

Tyrath unleashes a deafening, sky-splitting roar. The sound waves hit us like physical blows, vibrating through our chests and forcing us to clamp hands over our ears to keep from passing out.

With a furious jerk of his front claw, the dragon tears the bloodied icicle from his chest and teleports directly beside Kate's floating ice cage. Kate sways inside, pale and trembling from the strain of the cast, unable to raise a fresh shield in time.

Tyrath opens his jaws to incinerate the cage, but a lattice of brilliant golden light snaps into existence around him, sealing him inside a glowing cage. Dragon fire billows against the bars, but not a single flicker escapes; the flames wash harmlessly against the light and extinguish themselves.

"The fox's cage..." Tyrath snarls, his slit pupils narrowing.

He whips his head around to glare at Illuna.

"You cast this?"

"Your teleportation grew tedious," Illuna says, her voice chillingly calm. "You will remain still for the next two minutes."

"What does this achieve?" Tyrath sneers. "You cannot reach me through these bars any more than I can strike you. You only delay the inevitable."

"Not quite," Illuna replies.

Flower circles the golden cage on fiery thrusters, conjuring crackling spheres of flame and planting them suspended in the air, forming dense rings of floating fire around the prisoner.

"Let's see you swallow all of these at once, king of dragons," Flower taunts with a smirk.

Tyrath scoffs and begins weaving layers of defensive wards over his scales, bracing for the cage to drop. Beside them, Kate forces herself to shape three more steel-grade ice cones, her face pale and beaded with sweat. She is burning through her magical reserves at a dangerous rate.

There has to be something I can do from down here. I need a way into the sky.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Contraption, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Contraption)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Contraption
TEXT:

 Across the open arena, the deserted tiers fill with hundreds of royal guardsmen in dark blue tunics, hauling rune-inscribed ballistas and heavy spark-arbalests to the balustrades. Down on the sand, mechanics push three peculiar mechanical aircraft through the main sally port.

The contraptions resemble gnomish gyrocopters: twin overhead rotor blades, a slender tail boom, and canvas-covered glider wings. The main rotor runs on an air elementalist's turbine spell, leaving the rest of the craft purely mechanical.

Years ago on the Western Continent, an eccentric gnome engineer paid me in restricted magical manuscripts to test-pilot his experimental aircraft. One prototype's rotor seized at four hundred feet, forcing me to dead-stick glide into an oak canopy. I still wake up in a cold sweat thinking about those flights.

Near the service tunnel, a short, broad-shouldered gnome in royal regalia is screaming at a breathless crew chief. King Golmyck in the flesh.

"What do you mean the flight crews are absent?" Golmyck roars, waving a brass wrench. "I ordered three pilots and gunners stationed in the pens in case the manticores broke containment! Where are they?"

"The beasts died in the opening round, your majesty!" the chief stammers. "The crews went to the tavern. None of them are answering transceivers..."

"Damnations!" Golmyck curses. "I can take the stick myself, but I need hands on the turret!"

His sharp eyes snap toward our group on the sand.

"You there! Conscripts!" Golmyck points a finger. "Do any of you know how to handle gnomish flight controls? Or can anyone lead a target from a moving cockpit? I need crew right now!"

choice("Offer to pilot the aircraft", B2-Ch05b-Trapped, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Trapped, v_b2_ch5_pilot = 1)
choice("Offer to man the guns", B2-Ch05b-Trapped, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Trapped, v_b2_ch5_pilot = 0)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Trapped
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 1) {
 "I have logged hours on gnomish gyrocopters," I say, stepping forward. "I can fly it."

"Splendid!" Golmyck calls out. "Over here, Barry! Quick briefing."

"I will take the upper tiers," Daren says, broadsword in hand. "The guardsmen will need protective wards if they draw the beast's fire." He sprints for the stone steps.

"Listen closely," Golmyck says as I reach the cockpit. "Your flight controls."

He taps the center column.

"Control stick manages pitch and bank. Rudder pedals below are strictly for fine trim—do not kick the pedals to turn, or you will roll the craft. Bank with the stick."

"Understood."

"This lever on the left console," Golmyck points to a notched handle, "adjusts rotor blade pitch. Pull back to increase pitch and climb, but watch your airspeed. Push forward to drop altitude. Familiar?"

"Standard gnomish setup," I tell him. "I can manage."

"Excellent!" Golmyck beams. "Intuitive engineering pays off."

He glances up at the glowing golden cage holding Tyrath in the sky.

"Barry, how much time remains on that binding?"

"About two minutes."

"Plenty of room," Golmyck says. "Climb in. Strap down tight and pull these goggles over your eyes."

He tosses me a pair of tinted brass goggles. I clamber into the forward bucket seat and fasten the heavy leather harness. The tandem cockpit places the pilot and gunner back-to-back. Golmyck hops into the rear turret seat and snaps his own restraints.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 0) {
 "I haven't fired from a moving aircraft, but I know how to lead a target," I say, stepping forward.

"Splendid!" Golmyck calls out. "Over here, Barry! Quick briefing."

"I will take the upper tiers," Daren says, broadsword in hand. "The guardsmen will need protective wards if they draw the beast's fire." He sprints for the stone steps.

"Listen closely," Golmyck says as I reach the tail. "Your primary weapon."

He indicates the swivel mount seated at the base of the tail boom.

"Twin traverse handles for horizontal and vertical tracking. Thumb trigger on the right grip. The mechanism functions like a naval cannon, but loading runes automatically feed powder and shot into the breech. Aim and fire. Conserve ammunition—our supply is limited."

"Lead ball won't penetrate dragon hide," I point out.

"These are specialized alloy rounds," Golmyck replies. "Each slug is coated in concentrated acid. We know Tyrath's vulnerability. They will not drop him outright, but the caustic burn provides a vital distraction."

He glances up at the glowing golden cage holding Tyrath in the sky.

"Barry, how much time remains on that binding?"

"About two minutes."

"Plenty of room," Golmyck says. "Climb in. Strap down tight and pull these goggles over your eyes."

He tosses me a pair of tinted brass goggles. I strap into the rear gunner seat while Golmyck takes the pilot controls in front.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Cargo, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Cargo)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Cargo
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 1) {
 "So," I say over my shoulder as Golmyck checks the rear swivel turret. "I assume you have something heavier than lead shot for a dragon?"

"Specialized alloy rounds," Golmyck replies. "Coated in concentrated acid. They won't pierce his vitals, but the stinging burns will draw his attention away from your friends."

He reaches forward to tap a brass toggle on my instrument board.

"You have a forward nose gun as well. Press that rune toggle to discharge fixed bursts. Auto-loading enchantments handle the powder, so you only need to align the nose. Use it sparingly—those enchanted rounds cost a small fortune."

Golmyck signals the wind mage on the sand. The elementalist channels a turbine spell into the rotor hub, and the twin overhead blades blur into a deafening roar.

I pull back on the collective lever. The gyrocopter shudders, lifts from the arena floor, and climbs into the sky.

"You mentioned the guns are only a distraction!" I shout over the engine noise. "How do we actually bring him down?"

"We are not here to shoot him down!" Golmyck yells back. "Our objective is to maneuver directly above the dragon and deploy the snare!"

"The snare?"

"A heavy magical cable coiled in the ventral bomb bay!" Golmyck explains. "Once released, enchantment runes spin the rope outward into a weighted net. The cable is virtually unbreakable and designed to bind his wings to his flanks, forcing him into a dead fall. The complication is precision—Tyrath must remain stationary long enough for the net to settle. We cannot drop it immediately after the cage expires because the blast radius will scatter him. We have one cable and one hatch. We cannot afford a miss. Until the opening appears, we harry him with acid fire."

"And when he turns his flame breath on our cockpit?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 0) {
 Golmyck signals the wind mage on the sand. The elementalist channels a turbine spell into the rotor hub, and the twin overhead blades blur into a deafening roar.

Golmyck pulls back on the collective lever. The gyrocopter shudders, lifts from the arena floor, and climbs into the sky.

"You mentioned the guns are only a distraction!" I shout over the engine noise. "How do we actually bring him down?"

"We are not here to shoot him down!" Golmyck yells back. "Our objective is to maneuver directly above the dragon and deploy the snare!"

"The snare?"

"A heavy magical cable coiled in the ventral bomb bay!" Golmyck explains. "Once released, enchantment runes spin the rope outward into a weighted net. The cable is virtually unbreakable and designed to bind his wings to his flanks, forcing him into a dead fall. The complication is precision—Tyrath must remain stationary long enough for the net to settle. We cannot drop it immediately after the cage expires because the blast radius will scatter him. We have one cable and one hatch. We cannot afford a miss. Until the opening appears, we harry him with acid fire."

"And when he turns his flame breath on our cockpit?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Barrier, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Barrier)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Barrier
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 1) {
 "The airframe carries lessathi thermal dampening plating," Golmyck shouts back. "It provides moderate heat dissipation, but a direct blast of dragon fire will melt our controls in seconds. We must evade!"

We bank into an orbit above the coliseum. Ahead of us, the golden bars of Illuna's cage begin to flicker.

"Brace yourself!" Golmyck warns.

The golden lattice shatters. In that exact instant, every floating fireball Flower planted detonates in a single blinding concussive wave. The blast rattles our airframe, followed immediately by Kate's three steel-hard ice lances slamming into Tyrath's flank.

The defensive wards Tyrath layered over his scales absorb the brunt of the impact, but the shock visibly drains his immediate magical pool. His teleportation aura cuts out; he spreads his wings and takes to raw muscle flight.

Breaking through the smoke, Tyrath unleashes a torrent of flame at Kate's position. The distance gives Kate just enough room to bank her floating ice cage aside. Tyrath folds his wings to dive after her, but a synchronized lightning volley from the royal guardsmen in the stands strikes his chest, arcing across his scales.

Enraged, the dragon wheels around and blankets the spectator tiers in a deluge of fire. Daren steps to the stone balustrade, planting his heavy shield to project a shimmering white barrier that deflects the raging inferno.

Watching the beast's chest, the rhythm becomes clear. Before every breath attack, Tyrath draws an enormous breath, expanding his ribcage, and holds it for two full seconds while the internal flame ignites. Track the inhale, and you know the exact second the flame will erupt.

That tell gives me the window I need to outmaneuver him with the gyrocopter.

Tyrath snaps his attention toward Hadrik's eagle, diving in pursuit. The giant eagle is fast, but the dragon is closing the gap. Time to engage with the acid shot.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 0) {
 "The airframe carries lessathi thermal dampening plating," Golmyck shouts back. "It provides moderate heat dissipation, but a direct blast of dragon fire will melt our controls in seconds. We must evade!"

We bank into an orbit above the coliseum. Ahead of us, the golden bars of Illuna's cage begin to flicker.

"Brace yourself!" Golmyck warns.

The golden lattice shatters. In that exact instant, every floating fireball Flower planted detonates in a single blinding concussive wave. The blast rattles our airframe, followed immediately by Kate's three steel-hard ice lances slamming into Tyrath's flank.

The defensive wards Tyrath layered over his scales absorb the brunt of the impact, but the shock visibly drains his immediate magical pool. His teleportation aura cuts out; he spreads his wings and takes to raw muscle flight.

Breaking through the smoke, Tyrath unleashes a torrent of flame at Kate's position. The distance gives Kate just enough room to bank her floating ice cage aside. Tyrath folds his wings to dive after her, but a synchronized lightning volley from the royal guardsmen in the stands strikes his chest, arcing across his scales.

Enraged, the dragon wheels around and blankets the spectator tiers in a deluge of fire. Daren steps to the stone balustrade, planting his heavy shield to project a shimmering white barrier that deflects the raging inferno.

Watching the beast's chest, the rhythm becomes clear. Before every breath attack, Tyrath draws an enormous breath, expanding his ribcage, and holds it for two full seconds while the internal flame ignites. Track the inhale, and you know the exact second the flame will erupt.

"Golmyck!" I yell forward. "Watch his ribcage! He takes a massive lungful of air and holds it for two seconds to ignite the breath before he exhales! Track the inhale to dodge!"

"Good eye!" Golmyck calls back. "I will watch his chest."

Tyrath snaps his attention toward Hadrik's eagle, diving in pursuit. The giant eagle is fast, but the dragon is closing the gap. Time to engage with the acid shot.

}
choice("Attack the dragon", B2-Ch05b-Pilot, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Pilot) if (v_b2_ch5_pilot == 1)
choice("Wait for a better moment to attack", B2-Ch05b-Death, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Death)
choice("Attack the dragon", B2-Ch05b-Gunner, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Gunner) if (v_b2_ch5_pilot == 0)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Death
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 1) {
 I hold back on the throttle, waiting for a cleaner angle while Tyrath bears down on Hadrik.

The chase turns in a wide arc that brings the dragon directly across our flight path. Tyrath spots the gyrocopter, flares his wings to arrest his momentum, and glares straight into our cockpit.

"Insolent insect!" Tyrath roars. "You dare buzz around me in a mechanical toy? If you will not draw your magic, you die where you sit!"

His ribcage expands as he draws an enormous breath.

I jam the control stick forward, throwing the aircraft into a steep dive. The column of fire roars over our rotor blades, scorching our tail, but the dragon matches our dive instantly.

Move, you miserable pile of gears!

I bank hard left, but the mechanical craft cannot match the raw agility of beating dragon wings. Tyrath drops out of the sun, his massive rear talons smashing through our canopy and crushing me instantly.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 0) {
 I hold fire, waiting for a cleaner angle while Tyrath bears down on Hadrik.

The chase turns in a wide arc that brings the dragon directly across our flight path. Tyrath spots the gyrocopter, flares his wings to arrest his momentum, and glares straight into our cockpit.

"Insolent insect!" Tyrath roars. "You dare buzz around me in a mechanical toy? If you will not draw your magic, you die where you sit!"

His ribcage expands as he draws an enormous breath.

Golmyck jams the stick forward into a steep dive. The inferno whistles over our rotor, but the dragon swoops onto our tail in a heartbeat.

I traverse the turret and open fire, but Tyrath reads the barrel alignment, banking through the air to slip past the tracer line. The few acid rounds that graze his scales fail to slow his momentum.

"Can we outrun him?" I shout.

"Not a chance!" Golmyck yells back.

Golmyck wrenches the stick, but Tyrath swoops down with terrifying speed. His massive talons punch through the canopy, crushing me before I can pull the trigger again.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Pilot
TEXT:

 I bank the gyrocopter into a pursuit curve, aligning the nose with the dragon's starboard side.

"Engaging Tyrath!" I yell back. "Brace!"

"Understood!" Golmyck shouts.

The moment the crosshairs settle over his wing joint, I hammer the console button. A dozen acid-coated alloy slugs tear from the nose gun, punching into the thin membrane of the dragon's right wing.

Tyrath roars in agony as caustic burns hiss through his wing tissue. He banks hard to escape the fire, then whips his head toward us, ribcage swelling as he inhales deeply.

I read the tell, throwing the stick into a hard spiraling dive. Two seconds later, a torrent of dragon fire washes through the empty airspace above our rotors.

Tyrath swoops after us, but the acid-corroded wing drags in the wind, visibly slowing his top speed. Golmyck opens up with the rear turret, hammering suppressing bursts into his path to keep him off our tail.

Hadrik dives out of the clouds in eagle form, shifts to dwarf in mid-plummet, and smashes a heavy fist into the back of Tyrath's skull. He banks clear just as Kate's fresh ice lance punches through the dragon's shoulder.

Tyrath rips the icicle free and draws another desperate breath to incinerate the arena—

Instantly, the air around his head transfigures into a sphere of solid, churning water.

Tyrath inhales a full lungful of water. His eyes bulge. He thrashes violently, coughing and retching, but with every desperate gasp, he pulls more water into his burning lungs.

"Now!" Golmyck shouts. "Get us directly over his spine while he chokes!"

"On it!"

The dragon thrashes in mid-air, clawing at the water sphere engulfing his head.

"Struggling is futile," Illuna's voice drifts across the wind, icy and precise. "The sphere remains anchored. You cannot ignite flame with drowned lungs. Accept your defeat."

"How does it feel to drown in thin air, Tyrath?" Arraka cackles from the pendant. "I taught her that technique personally! All that grand posturing and you cannot even overcome my student! AHAHAHAHA!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Glowing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Glowing)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Gunner
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_combat_technique >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_combat_technique < 2)
#if(v_combat_technique >= 2) {
 

"Engaging Tyrath!" I shout forward. "Keep her steady!"

"Understood!" Golmyck calls back, locking the control column.

I grip the traverse handles, tracking the dragon's flight path. Accounting for our airspeed and the shudder of the airframe, I lead the target and squeeze the trigger.

Six acid-coated rounds hammer into the membrane of Tyrath's right wing. The caustic splash burns through scales and tissue, making the dragon shriek in pain and break his pursuit.

His chest heaves as he inhales to retaliate. Golmyck spots the expansion, throwing the craft into a dive just as the flames roar overhead.

I traverse the turret and lay down a second volley. The acid damage to his wing impairs his maneuverability, forcing him to bank wide.

Hadrik dives out of the clouds in eagle form, shifts to dwarf in mid-plummet, and smashes a heavy fist into the back of Tyrath's skull. He banks clear just as Kate's fresh ice lance punches through the dragon's shoulder.

Tyrath rips the icicle free and draws another desperate breath to incinerate the arena—

Instantly, the air around his head transfigures into a sphere of solid, churning water.

Tyrath inhales a full lungful of water. His eyes bulge. He thrashes violently, coughing and retching, but with every desperate gasp, he pulls more water into his burning lungs.

"Now!" Golmyck shouts. "I am positioning us directly above him! When I give the signal, pull the release lever on your right to drop the snare! Do you see it?"

"Understood!" I call back. "Awaiting your mark!"

The dragon thrashes in mid-air, clawing at the water sphere engulfing his head.

"Struggling is futile," Illuna's voice drifts across the wind, icy and precise. "The sphere remains anchored. You cannot ignite flame with drowned lungs. Accept your defeat."

"How does it feel to drown in thin air, Tyrath?" Arraka cackles from the pendant. "I taught her that technique personally! All that grand posturing and you cannot even overcome my student! AHAHAHAHA!"

}
#if(v_combat_technique < 2) {
 

"Engaging Tyrath!" I shout forward. "Keep her steady!"

"Understood!" Golmyck calls back, locking the control column.

I grip the traverse handles and open fire, but tracking a moving target from a vibrating airframe without proper technique proves disastrous. Half my rounds vanish into empty sky; the remainder ricochet harmlessly off his heavy breastplate instead of his wings.

Tyrath roars in fury and wheels toward us, his wing undamaged. His ribcage expands as he draws a breath.

Golmyck dives to evade the fire, but with full wing speed, the dragon overtakes us in seconds. My frantic bursts fail to deter him.

"Can we outrun him?" I yell.

"Not a chance!"

Tyrath swoops out of the sun, his massive rear talons smashing through the cockpit canopy and crushing me instantly.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Glowing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Glowing) if (v_combat_technique >= 2)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_combat_technique < 2)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_combat_technique < 2)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_combat_technique < 2)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Glowing
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 1) {
 Tyrath's slit pupils flare a searing crimson. Superheated steam billows from every crack in his scales as his internal body temperature spikes, hissing violently against the water sphere. He is flash-boiling the moisture in his chest to clear his lungs for another flame attack.

While the dragon superheats his body, Golmyck and I bank the gyrocopter into position directly above his neck, lining up the bomb hatch. The moment the water inside him evaporates, Tyrath exhales a violent concussive blast of fire that obliterates the remaining water sphere around his head.

"Dropping the cable!" Golmyck roars as the dragon gasps for fresh air.

He wrenches the release handle on his console, and the ventral cargo bay doors snap open.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_pilot == 0) {
 Tyrath's slit pupils flare a searing crimson. Superheated steam billows from every crack in his scales as his internal body temperature spikes, hissing violently against the water sphere. He is flash-boiling the moisture in his chest to clear his lungs for another flame attack.

While the dragon superheats his body, Golmyck and I bank the gyrocopter into position directly above his neck, lining up the bomb hatch. The moment the water inside him evaporates, Tyrath exhales a violent concussive blast of fire that obliterates the remaining water sphere around his head.

"Now!" Golmyck roars as the dragon gasps for fresh air.

"Releasing!" I shout back, yanking the heavy lever on my right console to open the ventral cargo hatch.

}
The enchanted cable drops through the air, uncoiling and spinning outward like a weighted flail.

Gasping for air, Tyrath fails to look up. The spinning cable strikes his shoulders, whipping tight around his torso and lashing his wings flush against his flanks.

The dragon lets out a strangled roar as his lift cuts out. He thrashes furiously, falling like a boulder toward the arena floor. He strains to snap the cord with raw wing power, then snaps at the cable with his fangs and rakes at it with his claws, but the enchanted strands hold fast. Twenty yards from the sand, he burns the last of his immediate mana into three rapid teleports to arrest his fall, but the downward momentum carries through.

Tyrath slams into the arena sand with a deafening crash, excavating a massive crater and sending a choking dust cloud over the tiers.

"The dragon is grounded!" Golmyck bellows to the entire coliseum. "Finish him now!"

"ENOUGH!"

A voice like grinding tectonic plates roars from the crater, accompanied by a blistering shockwave of heat that blows the dust cloud apart.

The beast that rises from the crater bears no resemblance to the dragon we fought. His scales have dissolved into flowing, incandescent molten rock, dripping white-hot slag onto the glassed sand. A fiery aura radiates from his frame—crushing, suffocating, dense enough to rival the presence of the golden fox.

Is this the primordial form Arraka spoke of? How can anyone hope to cross steel with something that burns like a small star?

The enchanted cable around his torso has already burned to ash. Tyrath spreads wings of solid magma and lifts into the sky.

"This humiliation is mine," Tyrath speaks, his voice eerily quiet. "I indulged the fantasy that insects posed a threat. I will rectify that error. I shall waste no further effort on single targets; a single incantation will reduce this city and every soul within its walls to glass."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Serious, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Serious)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Serious
TEXT:

 Tyrath draws his fiery aura inward, incandescent magma rippling across his chest as he begins to weave the incantation to incinerate the city.

The air across the coliseum warps in the blinding heat, but before the incantation can ignite, the dragon's molten frame violently shudders.

A concussive plume of scalding steam and caustic, sulfurous vapor blasts from the deep punctures in his chest and shoulders. The water from Illuna's drowning sphere, trapped and flash-boiled deep in his respiratory tract, violently clashes against the acid-etched wounds in his hide. A sickening hiss echoes across the arena as the bright magma along his flanks darkens into brittle, smoking obsidian.

He burned the last of his immediate mana pool on three emergency teleports and the sudden shift into primordial form. Maintaining a shroud that burns like a small star while channeling a city-leveling blast is cannibalizing his own core.

"Go on, oversized lizard!" Arraka's mocking cackle shrieks from the amulet below, dripping with pure spite. "Channel that continent-cracker! You burned through your immediate reservoir escaping a dwarf's cable trick, and your lungs are drenched in boiled water and caustic acid! Let's see if you manage to glass the city before your own chest cavity collapses from the thermal backlash!"

Tyrath's golden slit pupils flare with murderous hatred, but the incantation falters. The molten plating around his jaw fissures and cools to blackened rock as his depleted core struggles against the strain.

"What's the matter?" Arraka taunts him relentlessly. "Did the great 'King of Dragons' forget basic arcane backlash? Drop an unshielded primordial eruption right now and you'll vaporize your own vanguard in the foothills and leave yourself an empty husk on the sand! You don't have the reserves to survive your own blast!"

The dragon hovers, his fiery aura flickering violently against the sudden drain. The combined assault—Kate's ice lances, Hadrik's impacts, Illuna's drowning trap, and the gyrocopter's acid fire—has stripped away the exact margin of power he needed to level Thilias.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Rejoice, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Rejoice)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Rejoice
TEXT:

 Tyrath hovers in the sulfurous air, his slit golden eyes sweeping over the ruined sand, measuring the cost of his failing reserves.

"You have bought your lives by seconds, insects," Tyrath rumbles, his voice grinding like cooling tectonic plates. "I will not waste another spark of my breath on a ruined ring. But do not mistake this for survival. If I may not burn these cities with my own fire today, my dragon hordes will finish the work. I will unleash every legion across the northern peaks. I will not rest until every human settlement on Varathia is reduced to ash, whether it takes weeks or centuries. The next time our paths cross, everything you cling to will be rubble."

The dragon beats his colossal, smoking wings, climbing steeply into the upper cloud layer and streaking away toward the northern mountains.

The silence that falls over the arena is deafening. The air hangs heavy with sulfur, steam, and the stench of scorched sand.

High in the empty blue sky where the dragon vanished, a faint ripple passes through the air—not a physical descent, but a presence, like a light breeze carrying a quiet voice across the coliseum.

"Well, well," the disembodied voice muses, calm, melodic, and impossibly clear. "How terribly dramatic."

Arraka snorts from the amulet below. "Took your sweet time noticing the racket, didn't you, brat?"

"I was enjoying the performance," the unseen voice replies, airy and amused. "Though I suppose this complicates the trials. If the dragon king is mobilizing his hordes, the tournament organizers will have to delay the trials. And to keep things orderly... all mages registered in the tournament are temporarily classified as my followers under our ancient accord. A necessary formality to keep the dragon from devouring the contestants before the final rounds."

A pause, then the disembodied voice carries clearly down to the frozen royal guardsmen lining the balustrades, their faces white as parchment.

"What are you standing there for? Go on, spread the word across the kingdom! Rejoice, for your Creator has returned!"

The presence evaporates from the sky like morning mist, leaving only the whistling wind.

"Eiden!" a girl's voice rings out from the deserted spectator seats. "Hey, Eiden, can you hear me?"

I look toward the stone benches. A teenage girl in loose clothing stands alone in the middle tier, waving her arms and yelling toward the empty sky.

"Damn it!" she shouts, stomping her foot in irritation. "Don't you dare ignore me!"

Her voice echoes against the silent stone tiers, completely unanswered.

"I believe it is time we returned to solid ground, Barry," Golmyck says from behind me. "Our duty here is finished."

choice("Ask Arraka if Eiden is still hiding around here, invisibly", B2-Ch05b-Invisibly, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Invisibly)
choice("Don't ask Arraka anything", B2-Ch05b-Anything, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Anything)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Invisibly
TEXT:

 "Hey, Arraka," I call down. "Is Eiden lingering invisibly, or did he leave for good?"

"He's gone," Arraka snorts.

"Let's bring her down," I tell Golmyck.

I ease the collective lever forward, guiding the gyrocopter down toward the arena floor.

The skids touch down on the sand. Golmyck clambers out, shakes my hand with a firm grip, and heads off to rally his garrison, promising we will meet again at the palace.

Daren, Kate, Hadrik, and Flower gather near the aircraft. I wave across the dirt to Leila, who jogs over to join our circle. As we prepare to leave, Bruce steps up to offer his farewell.

"Fine battle, stillwater," Bruce says, clapping a heavy hand against my shoulder. "Fine battle. And the same to you, silver-haired princess. If fate ever throws us into another pit, I would welcome you on my flank. May our paths cross again."

"Small island," I tell him.

Leila and I bid him farewell before heading to the locker rooms to retrieve our backpacks. Geared and ready, our group makes for the main spectator archway.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Exit, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Exit)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Anything
TEXT:

 "Let's bring her down," I tell Golmyck.

I ease the collective lever forward, guiding the gyrocopter down toward the arena floor.

The skids touch down on the sand. Golmyck clambers out, shakes my hand with a firm grip, and heads off to rally his garrison, promising we will meet again at the palace.

Daren, Kate, Hadrik, and Flower gather near the aircraft. I wave across the dirt to Leila, who jogs over to join our circle. As we prepare to leave, Bruce steps up to offer his farewell.

"Fine battle, stillwater," Bruce says, clapping a heavy hand against my shoulder. "Fine battle. And the same to you, silver-haired princess. If fate ever throws us into another pit, I would welcome you on my flank. May our paths cross again."

"Small island," I tell him.

Leila and I bid him farewell before heading to the locker rooms to retrieve our backpacks. Geared and ready, our group makes for the main spectator archway.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Exit, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Exit)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Exit
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Come on," Daren says, leading the way. "I saw Enrique in the lower tiers during the fight; he must have evacuated with the crowd. He should be waiting outside the gates."

We step through the stone archway into the daylight, scanning the courtyard for Enrique. Before we can take ten paces, a teenage girl steps into our path with a bright, cheerful smile.

The same girl who was shouting at Eiden from the stands.

"Hello!" she greets us warmly. "I hope I am not intruding. I watched your combat in the pit—simply extraordinary! I was hoping you could assist me with a small matter."

"Apologies, young lady," Daren says, patting her shoulder briskly as he brushes past. "No autographs today."

He keeps walking toward Enrique, who waits near the fountain. The girl looks irritated by the brush-off, but she holds her ground.

Looking at her closely, her attire stands out. She wears loose, comfortable silk trousers and a matching tunic, resembling the nightwear sold across the Western Continent—the exact style Eiden favors.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Come on," Daren says, leading the way. "This is the gate Rose ran toward when the alarm sounded. She should be waiting outside."

We step through the stone archway into the daylight, scanning the courtyard. Before we can locate Rose, a teenage girl steps into our path with a bright, cheerful smile.

The same girl who was shouting at Eiden from the stands.

"Hello!" she greets us warmly. "I hope I am not intruding. I watched your combat in the pit—simply extraordinary! I was hoping you could assist me with a small matter."

"Apologies, young lady," Daren says, patting her shoulder briskly as he brushes past. "No autographs today."

He keeps walking toward Rose, who stands waiting near the fountain. The girl looks irritated by the brush-off, but she holds her ground.

Looking at her closely, her attire stands out. She wears loose, comfortable silk trousers and a matching tunic, resembling the nightwear sold across the Western Continent—the exact style Eiden favors.

}
She knows Eiden. She dresses like Eiden.

Could she be a—no, that is absurd. Another stillwater?

"Barry, are you coming?" Daren calls back.

"Right behind you," I answer, casting one last wary look at the girl before catching up.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch05b-Ending, v_current_scene = B2-Ch05b-Ending)


ID: B2-Ch05b-Ending
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "My friends!" Enrique beams, spreading his arms wide. "Magnificent! Unbelievable! Defeating the arena beasts was extraordinary enough, but driving off the king of dragons himself? And barely a scratch between you! We feast tonight at my estate. I will accept no refusals!"

"Excuse me, sir?" the girl in pajamas calls out, hurrying after Daren.

"Enrique, we appreciate the hospitality," Daren says, ignoring the girl behind him. "However, this arena detour already puts us three days behind the other tournament competitors. We cannot afford another night in Thilias."

"Excuse me? Sir?" the girl tries again.

"Don't be a killjoy, Daren!" Hadrik interjects with a laugh. "The organizers set no appointed hour for the initial trials. They won't disqualify us if we don't start hunting pine cones this very second."

"Hadrik, you are the last person who should speak on strategy!" Daren snaps. "You care nothing for the Magium—you just want an excuse to dodge the pine cone hunt!"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Thank the gods you are safe!" Rose says, rushing forward to clasp Kate's hands. "When the dragon appeared, the guards barred the gates. All I could see from the street was Kate, Hadrik, Flower, and that strange aircraft in the sky—I had no idea if you and Barry were even alive!"

"Excuse me, sir?" the girl in pajamas calls out, hurrying after Daren.

"Believe it or not," Daren says, gesturing toward me while ignoring the girl behind him, "Barry was piloting that craft, and the king was on the turret."

"Excuse me? Sir?" the girl tries again.

"King Golmyck?" Rose asks, wide-eyed.

"I'm beginning to see why the man won his crown in a landslide," Daren chuckles.

}
The air around Kate, Daren, and me seizes with sudden force.

Our boots lift from the paving stones. An invisible telekinetic grip hovers all three of us into the air and rotates us a clean hundred and eighty degrees.

Standing directly before us is the teenage girl in pajamas, her hands clasped, a bright, triumphant smile on her face.

"Well, then!" she says cheerfully, clapping her hands together. "Now that I have your undivided attention, perhaps we might have a little chat? I promise it won't take more than a few minutes!"

